WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.175
The cells of innate immunity.

2
00:00:02.225 --> 00:00:06.319
The exam asks where each cell comes from, how NK cells

3
00:00:06.369 --> 00:00:10.770
pick a target, and how mast cells differ from eosinophils.

4
00:00:11.133 --> 00:00:15.552
Every blood cell starts from one haematopoietic

5
00:00:15.602 --> 00:00:19.164
stem cell, or HSC, in the bone marrow.

6
00:00:19.214 --> 00:00:21.636
HSCs carry CD34 and c-KIT.

7
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:27.529
The myeloid branch makes neutrophils, eosinophils, basophils and monocytes.

8
00:00:27.579 --> 00:00:32.364
Most dendritic cells come from a precursor shared with monocytes.

9
00:00:32.733 --> 00:00:37.089
Mast cells leave the marrow as progenitors and mature in the tissues.

10
00:00:37.139 --> 00:00:40.856
Basophils mature in the marrow, from different progenitors.

11
00:00:41.233 --> 00:00:46.455
The lymphoid branch makes T cells, B cells, and a shared precursor

12
00:00:46.505 --> 00:00:51.168
of natural killer cells and innate lymphoid cells, or ILCs.

13
00:00:51.533 --> 00:00:58.978
Each line has a growth factor: G-CSF for neutrophils, M-CSF for monocytes,

14
00:00:59.028 --> 00:01:06.878
FLT3 ligand for dendritic cells, IL-5 for eosinophils, and SCF for mast cells.

15
00:01:07.233 --> 00:01:10.013
The neutrophil is the most abundant white

16
00:01:10.063 --> 00:01:12.981
blood cell, 40 to 60 percent of leukocytes.

17
00:01:13.031 --> 00:01:18.572
Its nucleus has three to five lobes, so we call it a polymorphonuclear leukocyte.

18
00:01:18.933 --> 00:01:24.646
Most granules are specific granules, with lysozyme, collagenase and elastase.

19
00:01:24.696 --> 00:01:29.959
Azurophilic granules hold myeloperoxidase, defensins and cathelicidins.

20
00:01:30.333 --> 00:01:33.598
Neutrophils act fast, with preformed enzymes,

21
00:01:33.648 --> 00:01:36.618
but live only one to two days in tissues.

22
00:01:36.668 --> 00:01:41.847
Macrophages respond more slowly, and often need new gene transcription.

23
00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:45.483
Neutrophils can throw out their nuclear contents

24
00:01:45.533 --> 00:01:48.260
as nets, neutrophil extracellular traps.

25
00:01:48.310 --> 00:01:52.703
Nets trap and kill microbes, but can also damage healthy tissue.

26
00:01:53.067 --> 00:01:57.233
Classical monocytes are 90 to 95 percent of blood monocytes.

27
00:01:57.283 --> 00:02:00.466
They rush to infection and become macrophages.

28
00:02:00.516 --> 00:02:04.049
But most tissue-resident macrophages come from yolk

29
00:02:04.099 --> 00:02:07.704
sac or fetal liver precursors, and renew themselves.

30
00:02:08.067 --> 00:02:11.333
Examples are Kupffer cells in the liver, alveolar

31
00:02:11.383 --> 00:02:14.786
macrophages in the lung and microglia in the brain.

32
00:02:15.167 --> 00:02:16.655
Two source conflicts.

33
00:02:16.705 --> 00:02:20.245
Janeway says tissue macrophages mature from blood

34
00:02:20.295 --> 00:02:24.567
monocytes, and dendritic cells also have a lymphoid origin.

35
00:02:24.617 --> 00:02:27.644
Abbas is newer, so use Abbas for the exam.

36
00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:31.976
Macrophages make cytokines such as TNF and IL-1.

37
00:02:32.026 --> 00:02:36.086
They clear apoptotic cells, called efferocytosis.

38
00:02:36.136 --> 00:02:42.544
Their receptor SIRP alpha reads CD47, a don't eat me signal on healthy cells.

39
00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:46.029
Lipopolysaccharide and interferon gamma give

40
00:02:46.079 --> 00:02:49.208
classical M1 macrophages that kill microbes.

41
00:02:49.258 --> 00:02:54.410
IL-4 and IL-13 drive alternative M2 macrophages for repair and fibrosis.

42
00:02:54.460 --> 00:02:56.794
Many macrophages fit neither box.

43
00:02:57.167 --> 00:02:59.590
Dendritic cells are sentinels.

44
00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:04.785
They capture antigens in tissues and carry them to lymph nodes.

45
00:03:04.835 --> 00:03:08.248
There, they direct naive T cells to become

46
00:03:08.298 --> 00:03:11.711
effector cells, such as Th1 or Th17 cells.

47
00:03:12.067 --> 00:03:19.892
cDC2 cells are the most numerous, and present antigen mainly to CD4 T cells.

48
00:03:19.942 --> 00:03:27.145
cDC1 cells cross-present antigen to naive CD8 T cells, and make IL-12.

49
00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:30.788
Plasmacytoid DCs carry high TLR7 and TLR9.

50
00:03:30.838 --> 00:03:35.239
They are the main source of antiviral type I interferon.

51
00:03:35.289 --> 00:03:39.849
Langerhans cells are the dendritic cells of the epidermis.

52
00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:44.023
Exam trap: follicular dendritic cells are stromal cells.

53
00:03:44.073 --> 00:03:49.693
They do not come from bone marrow, and do not present protein antigens to T cells.

54
00:03:50.067 --> 00:03:54.354
Natural killer cells are CD56 positive and CD3 negative.

55
00:03:54.404 --> 00:03:59.699
They kill without prior activation, using germline-encoded receptors.

56
00:03:59.749 --> 00:04:05.586
So they kill virus-infected cells before cytotoxic T cells are fully active.

57
00:04:05.967 --> 00:04:11.494
Perforin helps granzymes enter the target, and granzymes trigger apoptosis.

58
00:04:11.544 --> 00:04:16.552
NK cells also secrete interferon gamma, which activates macrophages.

59
00:04:16.933 --> 00:04:21.644
Each NK cell weighs activating against inhibitory signals.

60
00:04:21.694 --> 00:04:24.352
Think of it like a balance scale.

61
00:04:24.402 --> 00:04:29.851
Inhibitory receptors use ITIMs, and activating receptors use ITAMs.

62
00:04:30.233 --> 00:04:35.781
Inhibitory KIRs and CD94 NKG2A recognise MHC class I.

63
00:04:35.831 --> 00:04:44.336
The activating receptor NKG2D binds stress proteins on infected and tumour cells.

64
00:04:44.700 --> 00:04:51.419
A healthy cell shows MHC class I, so inhibition wins and the NK cell walks away.

65
00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:57.031
Viruses, cell stress and many cancers reduce MHC class I.

66
00:04:57.081 --> 00:05:02.312
The brake is gone, the scale tips, and the NK cell kills.

67
00:05:02.362 --> 00:05:05.647
This is recognition of missing self.

68
00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:12.754
CD16 binds IgG1 and IgG3 on a coated cell, and the NK cell kills it.

69
00:05:12.804 --> 00:05:19.058
This is antibody-dependent cell-mediated cytotoxicity, or ADCC.

70
00:05:19.433 --> 00:05:25.397
NK cell deficiency gives severe herpesvirus and papillomavirus infections.

71
00:05:25.447 --> 00:05:31.655
Causes include GATA2 mutations, CD16 mutations, and Chediak-Higashi syndrome.

72
00:05:32.033 --> 00:05:36.898
Now the allergy trio: mast cells, basophils and eosinophils.

73
00:05:36.948 --> 00:05:40.338
All three have granules full of mediators,

74
00:05:40.388 --> 00:05:43.941
and all three take part in allergic disease.

75
00:05:44.300 --> 00:05:46.451
Mast cells are not in the blood.

76
00:05:46.501 --> 00:05:50.302
They sit near vessels and nerves, and beneath epithelia.

77
00:05:50.352 --> 00:05:54.978
Stem cell factor binds c-KIT and is essential for their development.

78
00:05:55.333 --> 00:06:00.097
The high-affinity IgE receptor, F C epsilon R one, holds IgE so

79
00:06:00.147 --> 00:06:05.064
tightly that most mast cells carry IgE, even in nonatopic people.

80
00:06:05.114 --> 00:06:09.877
It is high on mast cells and basophils, but low on eosinophils.

81
00:06:10.233 --> 00:06:12.233
Allergen cross-links the IgE.

82
00:06:12.283 --> 00:06:16.262
The mast cell releases histamine and tryptase, then makes

83
00:06:16.312 --> 00:06:20.644
prostaglandin D2, leukotrienes and platelet-activating factor.

84
00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:24.142
Mast cells also fire without IgE.

85
00:06:24.192 --> 00:06:27.237
C3a and C5a cause degranulation.

86
00:06:27.287 --> 00:06:31.880
On skin mast cells, MRGPRX2 binds some drugs and

87
00:06:31.930 --> 00:06:36.426
likely causes some allergy-like drug reactions.

88
00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:41.635
Tryptase is in all human mast cells and no other known cell.

89
00:06:41.685 --> 00:06:45.299
So serum tryptase helps diagnose anaphylaxis.

90
00:06:45.349 --> 00:06:49.451
Systemic mastocytosis is linked to c-KIT mutations.

91
00:06:49.833 --> 00:06:55.032
Basophils are blood granulocytes, normally absent from tissues.

92
00:06:55.082 --> 00:07:00.197
Like mast cells, they bind IgE and fire when antigen binds it.

93
00:07:00.567 --> 00:07:04.798
Eosinophils need IL-5 from Th2 cells and ILC2s.

94
00:07:04.848 --> 00:07:08.168
Eotaxin binds CCR3 and recruits them.

95
00:07:08.218 --> 00:07:15.819
Major basic protein and eosinophil cationic protein damage helminths and host cells.

96
00:07:16.200 --> 00:07:22.440
Exam trap: the eosinophil IgE receptor is low and lacks the signalling chain.

97
00:07:22.490 --> 00:07:27.586
There is no convincing evidence that IgE activates eosinophils.

98
00:07:27.967 --> 00:07:31.210
Innate lymphoid cells have no T-cell receptors.

99
00:07:31.260 --> 00:07:34.503
They secrete cytokines like helper T cells, and

100
00:07:34.553 --> 00:07:37.936
live mainly in mucosa such as lung and intestine.

101
00:07:38.300 --> 00:07:42.418
ILC1s use T-bet and make interferon gamma.

102
00:07:42.468 --> 00:07:46.387
ILC2s use GATA3 and make IL-5 and IL-13.

103
00:07:46.437 --> 00:07:50.951
ILC3s use ROR gamma t and make IL-22 or IL-17.

104
00:07:51.333 --> 00:07:58.267
In allergy, epithelial IL-25, IL-33 and TSLP activate ILC2s.

105
00:07:58.317 --> 00:08:04.087
ILC2s already sit in the tissue, so they can start

106
00:08:04.137 --> 00:08:09.441
allergic inflammation before Th2 cells arrive.

107
00:08:09.800 --> 00:08:11.634
Five things to remember.

108
00:08:11.684 --> 00:08:14.773
One: all innate cells come from the HSC.

109
00:08:14.823 --> 00:08:19.169
Two: most tissue macrophages come from fetal precursors.

110
00:08:19.219 --> 00:08:22.858
Three: plasmacytoid DCs make type I interferon.

111
00:08:23.233 --> 00:08:28.549
Four: NK cells kill when MHC class I is missing.

112
00:08:28.599 --> 00:08:33.690
Five: ILC2s start allergic inflammation early.

113
00:08:34.067 --> 00:08:35.862
One exam trap.

114
00:08:35.912 --> 00:08:40.608
CD16 on NK cells binds IgG, not IgE.

115
00:08:40.658 --> 00:08:44.431
Next: how cells sense danger.
